              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
          %h2#goal Goal
          %p
            The goal of this page is to prove that a non-extensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/choice+operator"} choice operator
            is conservative over a theory without AC. I’ll take the theory without AC to be
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SEAR"} SEAR
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"} I'm
            most comfortable.
          %h2#defining_sear
            Defining SEAR+
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon
          %p
            For the theory with a non-extensional choice operator, consider the following theory, which is a variant of the version of SEAR without fundamental equality described at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SEAR"} SEAR
            = succeed "," do
              %em pre-sets
            = succeed "," do
              %em pre-relations
            = succeed "," do
              %em elements
            %em operations
            (or
            = succeed ")." do
              %em pre-functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ↬
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi:A\looparrowright B
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\in B
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(x,y)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⇝
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\rightsquigarrow B
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in A
            we have an element
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)\in B
            %em definition
            of when two parallel pre-relations are called “equal”. We also impose
          .un_prop
            %h6#axiom__choice_operator
              Axiom
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mi ε
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1+\varepsilon
              (Choice operator)
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi φ
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ↬
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi:A\looparrowright B
              is a pre-relation such that for every
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in A
              there exists a
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\in B
              with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi φ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi(x,y)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ε
                      %mi φ
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⇝
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon_\varphi :A\rightsquigarrow B
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ε
                      %mi φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi(x,\varepsilon_\varphi(x))
              for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in A
          %p
            A
            %em set
            is defined to be a pre-set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            equipped with an equivalence pre-relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} =_A
            which satisfies the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+substitution"} principle of substitution
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+of+indiscernibles"} identity of indiscernibles
            %em relation
            between sets is a pre-relation which is
            = succeed "," do
              %em extensional
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi(x,y)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi A
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x'=_A x
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ′
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi B
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y'=_B y
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi(x',y')
            %em function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\to B
            between sets is an operation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⇝
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\rightsquigarrow B
            which is extensional, i.e. if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ′
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x' =_A x
            then
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi B
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x')=_B f(x)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g:A\to B
            to be
            %em equal
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi B
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)=_B g(x)
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in A
          %p
            For any operation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⇝
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\rightsquigarrow B
            between
            = succeed "," do
              %em sets
            = succeed ")" do
              %em graph
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⇔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi B
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_f(x,y) \Leftrightarrow (f(x)=_B y)
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entire+relation"} entire
            in the sense that for any
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\in B
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_f(x,y)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            unless
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is actually a function (in which case
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Γ
                    %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_f
            is a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+relation"} functional relation
            in the usual sense). Conversely, Axiom
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1+\varepsilon
            says that any entire pre-relation induces an operation, and for an entire and functional relation between sets, this induced operation is a function (and is unique, in the sense of equality of functions defined above).
          %p
            Now Axiom 2 can be taken to read: For any sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B
            and any relation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi φ
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ↬
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi:A\looparrowright B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\varphi|
            and operations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ⇝
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:{|\varphi|}\rightsquigarrow A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo :
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ⇝
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q:{|\varphi|}\rightsquigarrow B
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi(x,y)
            iff there exists
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo ∈
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z\in {|\varphi|}
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(z)=_A x
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi B
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q(z)=_B y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi φ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi z
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z=_{|\varphi|} z'
            iff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(z)=_A p(z')
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi B
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q(z)=_B q(z')
            to make
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\varphi|
            into a set and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p,q
            into functions that are jointly injective. Axioms 3, 4, and 5 are easy to translate.
          %p
            We call this theory
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SEAR
                    %mo +
                    %mi ε
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SEAR+\varepsilon
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SEAR
                  %mo +
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SEAR+\varepsilon
            satisfy the axioms of SEAR. Conversely, from any model of SEAR-C we can construct a model of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SEAR
                  %mo +
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SEAR+\varepsilon
            by taking the pre-sets, pre-relations, and operations to be the SEAR-C sets, relations, and functions respectively. With this interpretation axiom
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1+\varepsilon
            is precisely the SEAR-C
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SEAR
                  %mo +
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SEAR+\varepsilon
            without assuming or implying choice.
          %h2#conservativity_over_coshep Conservativity over COSHEP
          %p
            Our goal is to prove that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SEAR
                  %mo +
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SEAR+\varepsilon
            is conservative over SEAR. But since I haven’t quite figured out how to do that (or, to be fair, even whether it’s true), as a warm-up let’s prove that the same thing is true when we add
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/COSHEP"} COSHEP
          %p
            Suppose we have a model of SEAR satisfying COSHEP, call it
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+object"} projective
            one. We define a model of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SEAR
                  %mo +
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SEAR+\varepsilon
            as follows.
          %ul
            %li
              The pre-sets are the projective
              = succeed "-sets." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %li
              The elements are the
              = succeed "-elements." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %li
              The pre-relations are the
              = succeed "-relations." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %li
              The operations are the
              = succeed "-functions." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            We will continue to qualify the notions in the old model
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            Axiom 0 of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SEAR
                  %mo +
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SEAR+\varepsilon
            is obviously true. For Axiom 1, we need to translate a first-order formula in the language of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SEAR
                  %mo +
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SEAR+\varepsilon
            into a formula in the language of SEAR in order to apply Axiom 1 of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1+\varepsilon
            is true because we have chosen the pre-sets to be the
            %em projective
            sets in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "-relation" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "-function." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            Now a
            %em set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            in our putative model of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SEAR
                  %mo +
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SEAR+\varepsilon
            is a
            = succeed "-set" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} =_A
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo =
                        %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A/{=_A}
            %em relation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ↬
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\looparrowright B
            = succeed "-relation" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo =
                        %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↬
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo =
                        %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A/{=_A}) \looparrowright (B/{=_B})
            = succeed "-relation" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ↬
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\looparrowright B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to B
            and
            = succeed "-functions" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo =
                        %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo =
                        %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A/{=_A}) \to (B/{=_B})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Set
                    %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set\to Set_V
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Rel
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Rel
                    %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Rel\to Rel_V
            given by “take quotients” that are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} fully faithful
          %p
            I claim that in fact these functors are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+surjective+functor"} essentially surjective
            as well. For given any
            = succeed "-set" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            and a surjection
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo :
                    %mi P
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e:P\to A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Set
                    %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_V
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"} regular category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+set"} quotient set
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel+pair"} kernel pair
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} =_P
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            becomes a new-style set which maps onto
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            under the quotient functor; hence this functor is essentially surjective.
          %p
            It follows that the meta-categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Rel
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Rel
            (in the new sense) are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
            to the old meta-categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Set
                    %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_V
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Rel
                      %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Rel_V
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            Thus, we have constructed a model of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SEAR
                    %mo +
                    %mi ε
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SEAR+\varepsilon
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            %em in the language of SEAR
            which are true in this new model of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SEAR
                  %mo +
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SEAR+\varepsilon
            are precisely those which are true in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            Therefore, any statement in the language of SEAR which is true in all models of SEAR that underlie models of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SEAR
                  %mo +
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SEAR+\varepsilon
            is, in fact, true in
            %em all
            models of SEAR+COSHEP. This is the conservativity result we were looking for. Its practical upshot is that if you want to work in SEAR+COSHEP, you might as well work in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SEAR
                  %mo +
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SEAR+\varepsilon
            if it’s more convenient to have a choice operator, since the theorems you can prove in either case will be exactly the same.
          %p
            Note that the model of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SEAR
                  %mo +
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SEAR+\varepsilon
            we’ve just constructed also satisfies COSHEP (as it must, given its equivalence to
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SEAR
                  %mo +
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SEAR+\varepsilon
            admits an “identity” equivalence pre-relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ≡
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \equiv_A
            on every pre-set, and relative to which all pre-relations and operations are extensional, then it must satisfy COSHEP—for then every set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,{=_A})
            is the surjective image of the set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ≡
                        %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,{\equiv_A})
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mi ε
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1+\varepsilon
          %h2#without_coshep Without COSHEP
          %p
            Now
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Toby+Bartels"} I
            step in to say:
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi SEAR
                    %mo +
                    %mi ε
                    %mo ⊨
                    %mi COSHEP
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{SEAR} + \varepsilon \vDash COSHEP
          %p
            The reason is that every preset
            %em does
            admit an identity prerelation as in the last paragraph above; let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ≡
                    %mi A
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \equiv_A y
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi R
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \sim_R y
            for every reflexive prerelation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ↬
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R: A \looparrowright A
            (or even for every equivalence prerelation). This will work also in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi ETCS
                  %mo −
                  %mi AC
                  %mo +
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{ETCS} - AC + \varepsilon
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantification"} quantifying
            over prefunctions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: A \to \mathcal{P}1
            and using the kernel of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            (relative to the standard equality on truth values) as the equivalence relation. (Of course,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi SEAR
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{SEAR}
            is capable of using this method too.) It will still work in versions with intuitionistic logic, but not (as far as I can see) in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi CETCS
                  %mo −
                  %mi COSHEP
                  %mo +
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{CETCS} - COSHEP + \varepsilon
            (following
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.uu.se/~palmgren/cetcs.pdf"} Palmgren
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
            \: Ah, you’re right. I actually thought of that briefly, but then I didn’t immediately see how to prove the following.
          #equiv-exten.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              Define
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %msub
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi A
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \equiv_A y
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R(x,y)
              for every equivalence pre-relation
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ↬
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R: A \looparrowright A
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Suppose
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⇝
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\rightsquigarrow B
              is an operation. Define
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ↬
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_f:A\looparrowright A
              by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_f(x,x')
              iff
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mo ≡
                        %mi B
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)\equiv_B f(x')
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              is an equivalence pre-relation, so if
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %msub
                        %mo ≡
                        %mi A
                      %mi x
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\equiv_A x'
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi K
                        %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_f(x,x')
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mo ≡
                        %mi B
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)\equiv_B f(x')
            %p
              Similarly, suppose
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi φ
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ↬
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi:A\looparrowright B
              is a pre-relation. Define
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mi φ
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ↬
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_\varphi:A\looparrowright A
              by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mi φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_\varphi(x,x')
              iff
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⇔
                    %mi φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi(x,y)\Leftrightarrow\varphi(x',y)
              for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\in B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mi φ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_\varphi
              is an equivalence pre-relation, so if
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %msub
                        %mo ≡
                        %mi A
                      %mi x
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\equiv_A x'
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mi φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_\varphi(x,x')
              and so
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⇔
                    %mi φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi(x,y)\Leftrightarrow\varphi(x',y)
              for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\in B
          %p
            Of course, as you point out, this is very impredicative. Does that mean that your original
            %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2009/09/towards_a_computeraided_system.html#c026817"} suggestion
            would only acceptable be for someone who either (1) accepts COSHEP or (2) doesn’t accept (quantification over) powersets?
          %p
            %em> Toby
            \: That may be so; I hadn't thought through all of the implications, so I was hoping otherwise. However, I'm not sure that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon
            has to work as in Axiom
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mi ε
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 + \varepsilon
          .un_prop
            %h6#axiom__global_choice_operator
              Axiom
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ε
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon
              (Global Choice Operator)
            %p
              Add a symbol (actually several symbols) to the language for an operation that assigns to each set
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %ul
              %li
                a set
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ′
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A'
              %li
                an injective function
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi i
                          %mi A
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                        %mo →
                        %mi A
                        %mo ′
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_A: A \to A'
              %li
                and an element
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon_A
                of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ′
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A'
            %p
              then add the axiom that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon_A
              belongs to the image of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_A
              if (hence iff)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is inhabited.
          %p
            With excluded middle, this is a theorem with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mn 0
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A + 0^A
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A'
            as in the discussion at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/choice+operator"} choice operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon_A
            takes values in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\bot
            (the set of subsingleton subsets of
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon_A
            an inhabited subsingleton if (hence iff)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is inhabited, but the existence of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\bot
            is itself impredicative (in the presence of function sets) since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_\bot
            is the set of truth values.
          %h2#without_coshep_predicatively Without COSHEP, predicatively
          %p
            Okay, second try. Define
            %strong constructive SEAR
            to use
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic logic
            and consist of Axioms 0, 1B, 2, and 4 of SEAR (no power sets) together with
          %ul
            %li
              The existence of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+sets"} quotient sets
              (i.e.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              is an
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+category"} exact category
            %li
              The existence of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+unions"} disjoint unions
              (i.e. now
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              is a
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretopos"} pretopos
            %li
              The existence of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+products"} dependent products
              (i.e. now
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              is a
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CE%A0-pretopos"} pretopos
          %p
            Now modify it as above, removing basic equality and adding a basic notion of “operation,” to obtain
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                CSEAR+
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ε
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon
          %p
            The same argument as above should show that CSEAR+
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon
            is conservative over CSEAR+COSHEP. (Just as Bounded SEAR is equivalent to ETCS, CSEAR+COSHEP is equivalent to Palmgren’s CETCS.) However, the converse argument above fails since we cannot define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ≡
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \equiv_A
            by quantifying over relations (note that avoiding this requires not only throwing out powersets, but restricting axiom 1 to
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+quantification"} bounded quantification
          %p
            Now: is CSEAR+
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon
            conservative over CSEAR?
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS+plus+epsilon"} ETCS plus epsilon
